Java code for measuring execution time
Add this method inside the Triage class to measure insertion and removal costs.

static void growthTest() {

    int[] sizes = {100, 1000, 10000};

    for (int n : sizes) {

        PriorityQueue<Patient> q =
            new PriorityQueue<>(
                Comparator.comparingInt(
                    (Patient p) -> p.severity
                ).thenComparingLong(p -> p.arrival)
            );

        long start = System.nanoTime();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            q.offer(new Patient("Patient" + i,
                       (i % 5) + 1, i));
        }

        long addTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        start = System.nanoTime();

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            q.poll();
        }

        long removeTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        System.out.println("Patients: " + n);
        System.out.println("Add time: " + addTime + " ns");
        System.out.println("Remove time: " + removeTime + " ns");
        System.out.println();
    }
}



Call growthTest(); from a separate main() method or temporarily from the existing main method.

Expected experimental observation: As the queue size increases, total insertion and removal time generally increases. However, the average cost per operation grows approximately logarithmically.

Note: Actual nanosecond measurements depend on the computer, JVM warm-up, garbage collection and system load. Record the values produced by your own execution rather than treating theoretical predictions as measured results.
10. Starvation Problem and Fairness Rule
What is starvation?

Starvation occurs when a low-priority patient waits for an unlimited amount of time because higher-priority patients continuously arrive.

For example, a severity-5 patient may never receive treatment if new severity-1 and severity-2 patients keep entering the hospital.
Proposed solution: Priority Aging
Priority aging means increasing a patient's priority as their waiting time increases.

Proposed rule:
- Severity 1–4 patients follow normal priority.
- If a severity-5 patient waits longer than 30 minutes, promote their effective priority to severity 4.
- If the patient continues waiting, their priority can be increased further according to hospital policy.
- Critical patients must still receive immediate attention when medically necessary.
Example:
Patient	Original severity	Waiting time	Effective priority
Ravi	5	10 minutes	5
Meena	5	35 minutes	4
Kumar	3	5 minutes	3

Meena receives a higher priority than other severity-5 patients because she has waited longer.

Implementation consideration: A comparator that directly depends on the current time can invalidate the heap's ordering when time passes. Therefore, the hospital should periodically recalculate effective priorities and rebuild or reinsert the waiting patients into the priority queue. A scheduled aging mechanism is one possible implementation.
11. Alternative Concepts and Complexity Analysis
Three possible queue implementations are compared below.
Operation	PriorityQueue	Sorted LinkedList	Five FIFO queues
Admit patient	O(log n)	O(n)	O(1)
Serve next patient	O(log n)	O(1)	O(1)
Space	O(n)	O(n)	O(n)
Priority handling	Comparator	Sorted insertion	Separate severity queues

Method 1: PriorityQueue
- Uses a binary heap.
- Adds patients based on severity and arrival number.
- Removes the highest-priority patient using poll().
- Both admission and removal take O(log n).
Method 2: Sorted LinkedList
- Maintains patients in sorted order.
- Each new patient is inserted into the correct position.
- Insertion requires searching through the list, so it takes O(n).
- Removing the first patient takes O(1).
Method 3: Five FIFO Queues
Create five separate queues:
- Queue 1 – Severity 1
- Queue 2 – Severity 2
- Queue 3 – Severity 3
- Queue 4 – Severity 4
- Queue 5 – Severity 5
Admission takes O(1) because the patient is inserted into the corresponding FIFO queue.

To serve the next patient, check queues from severity 1 to severity 5 and remove the first available patient.

Since there are only five queues, checking them takes at most five operations, which is O(1).

Best choice: Five FIFO queues are efficient for this particular problem because there are exactly five fixed severity levels. However, PriorityQueue is more flexible when priorities or ranking rules become more complex.
